[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/vZQa2yT6"]
[FEN "8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Rook endings: everybody reaches them, almost nobody knows them. First rule: your rook belongs behind the passed pawn, on its own column, pushing it from the back. Go to d4. [%cal Ya4d4]}
1. Rd4 {Rd4 tucks your rook in behind the pawn, exactly where it belongs. Nothing on the board can stop it now. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd6d8]} (1. d7 {Running the pawn to d7 alone is the mistake: with no rook behind it, his rook simply blocks it in front, on d5.} 1... Rd5 2. d8=Q+ Rxd8 {Your new queen lasts one move: his rook takes her straight back on d8. Drawn.}) (1. Ra6+ {Ra6+ is a free check, but it hangs your rook for nothing: he simply takes it.} 1... Kxa6 {The rook is gone, taken for nothing on a6, and the pawn falls next. Lost.}) (1. Rb4 {Offering the trade with Rb4 delights him: he takes it at once, and your pawn stands alone.} 1... Rxb4+ {Now the pawn on d6 has nobody left to guard it, and it falls. You are lost.}) (1. Ra5 {Ra5 goes sideways and lands your rook right beside his, where it simply gets taken.} 1... Rxa5 {The rook is gone, traded for nothing on a5, and the pawn is left defenceless. You are lost.}) (1. Ra8 {Ra8 is far from the pawn and far from safe: it does nothing useful sitting on the back row.} 1... Rb4+ 2. Kf3 Rb3+ {His checks never stop from there, and your king can never escape them. Drawn.}) 1... Kc5 {He comes after your rook on d4. Push the pawn on to d7 regardless of the threat.} 2. d7 {Your rook shields the pawn from behind, and it queens no matter what he tries. Now watch it go home. [%csl Gd7][%tail]} (2. Rd1 {Rd1 also wins: it is still behind the pawn, just watching from further back down the column.} 2... Kc6 {The pawn walks home just the same from there, with the rook still shielding it from checks.}) (2. Rd2 {Rd2 also wins: the rook drops back two squares and still stands behind the pawn on d6.} 2... Kc6 {Behind the pawn is behind the pawn, wherever exactly you stand on the d column. Still winning.}) (2. Rd3 {Rd3 is also fine: any square directly behind the pawn on the d column does the job.} 2... Kc6 {The pawn walks home from here too, since the rook behind it never lets a check through.}) (2. Rd5+ {Rd5+ is a check, but his king simply captures the rook you have just handed him.} 2... Kxd5 3. d7 Rb8 {His rook swings round to b8 and covers d8, so the pawn can never queen. A rook against a pawn: you are lost. [%cal Rb8d8]}) (2. Rc4+ {Rc4+ puts the rook right next to his king on c5, and a check does not stop him taking it.} 2... Kxc4 3. Kf3 Rd5 {The pawn on d6 falls next with no rook left to defend it. You are lost.}) (2. Ra4 {Ra4 abandons the pawn completely on d6, leaving it to fend for itself against his king.} 2... Kxd6 {His king simply walks up and eats the undefended pawn on d6. Drawn.}) 2... Kxd4 3. d8=Q+ {A new queen appears on d8, with check, and his king has just taken your rook. Queen against rook is a win, and the rook behind the pawn made it. [%csl Gd8]} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: get behind his runner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/5haHjaqz"]
[FEN "4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7_w_-_-_0_1"]

{He is a pawn up, and his king already sits on d6, ready to escort that pawn home. A draw is a fine result here: can you hold it?}
1. e5 {The pawn sets off, pushing to e5. How do you stop it?} 1... Re2 {Re2 lines up behind the pawn on the e column. His king now has to guard it every single step, since your rook is ready to grab it free the moment he looks away.} (1... Rd2+ {Rd2+ only helps him: the check drives his king exactly where it wants to go.} 2. Ke6 Kd8 3. Ra8+ {You are driven back off the d column, and the pawn walks straight in behind you. Lost.}) (1... Rh6+ {Rh6+ checks from the side, and his pawn simply blocks it on e6 and moves forward.} 2. e6 Kf8 3. Ra8+ {Lost. The check on a8 drives your king off the back row, and the pawn on e6 walks in.}) (1... Kf7 {Stepping aside with Kf7 sets the pawn free to run all the way to e8 unopposed.} 2. e6+ Kf6 3. Rf1+ {Lost. His rook checks your king away from f6, and nothing stands in front of the pawn on e6 any more.}) (1... Ra2 {Offering the trade with Ra2 is one you cannot afford: without rooks, his pawn simply runs.} 2. Rxa2 {A bare king against a pawn and a king now, with nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (1... Rh1 {Rh1 wanders off into the corner, and the rook is not even safe once it gets there.} 2. Rxh1 {Lost. Your rook is gone for nothing, and the pawn walks in right behind it.}) 2. e6 {He pushes past your rook, to e6. Now which check does your rook have left to give?} 2... Rd2+ {Check from the d column, and his king has to leave d6. Your rook stays behind his pawn, your king sits in its road on e8, so now watch his try fail. [%csl Gd2][%cal Ge8e6][%tail]} (2... Rxe6+ {Taking the free pawn with Rxe6+ looks great, but look at what is left on the board after it.} 3. Kxe6 {A bare king against his king and rook now, with nothing to hold them off. You are lost.}) (2... Re1 {Re1 stays behind the pawn but gives no check, and your back row is left open.} 3. Ra8# {Mate! He could have taken your rook on e1, but mate is better: his king on d6 and pawn on e6 cover every escape square.}) (2... Kf8 {Kf8 steps your king off the e column, right when it needed to stand in front of the pawn.} 3. Ra8+ {And the pawn queens right behind you on e8, with your king facing the wrong way. Lost.}) 3. Ke5 3... Ke7 4. Ra7+ 4... Ke8 5. e7 5... Rd7 {Rd7 offers the trade of rooks. His king on e5 is too far from e8 to guard the queening square.} 6. Rxd7 6... Kxd7 7. e8=Q+ 7... Kxe8 {A new queen appears on e8, and your king eats her at once. Two bare kings on an empty board: a draw. [%csl Ge8]} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: your rook is attacked"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/Gi4bWJcb"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His king is attacking your rook on b4, and your pawn on e3 is on its own. Find a square for the rook.}
1... Re4 {Re4 tucks in behind your own pawn, out of his king's reach. Now the pawn can never be blocked from the front. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge3e1]} (1... e2 {Racing the pawn to e2 alone runs it straight into his rook, with nothing there to help it.} 2. Rxe2+ Kd7 3. Kxb4 {Your pawn on e2 is gone, and now your rook falls to his king too. Lost.}) (1... Ra4+ {Ra4+ is a check, but the rook lands right where his king can simply take it.} 2. Kxa4 {The rook is gone, and the game goes with it, since the pawn has nobody left. Lost.}) (1... Rb3+ {Rb3+ checks from right in front of his king, on a square nothing of yours guards.} 2. Kxb3 {Lost. His king simply steps up and captures the rook that just checked it.}) (1... Rb2 {Rb2 guards the pawn from the side, but it stands right next to his own king.} 2. Kxb2 {Lost. The rook stood beside his king instead of behind your pawn, and now it is simply gone.}) (1... Rf4 {Rf4 also dodges the check, but off the e column your rook cannot shield the pawn on e3 from behind.} 2. Re2 {He drops his rook in front of the pawn again, sitting right on e2.} 2... Rf3 {Rf3 guards the pawn from the side instead, but now it can never leave that square again.} 3. Kb3 {His king marches back with nothing left to stop it, since your rook is stuck for good. Only a draw.}) (1... Kd6 {Kd6 brings the king over to help, but it leaves the rook on b4 hanging for free.} 2. Kxb4 {Lost. His king scoops up the unguarded rook on b4, and the pawn falls next.}) 2. Re2 {He blocks right in front of the pawn on e2. It cannot move by itself now, so who can move it forward?} 2... Ke6 {Ke6 starts your king toward the pawn on e3. Your king is close enough to beat his king there, and nothing else on the board can push the pawn through. [%csl Ge6]} (2... Kd6 {Kd6 is also good: any step toward the pawn on e3 does the exact same job.} 3. Kb2 Ke5 {Still winning: your king arrives at the pawn on e3 just the same, one way or another.}) (2... Kf6 {Kf6 is also fine: it reaches the pawn on e3 just as fast as the others.} 3. Kb2 Ke5 {Still winning: your king reaches the pawn on e3 just as well starting from here.}) 3. Kb2 {His king runs back toward e3 to help defend it. Keep your own king marching in.} 3... Ke5 {Straight at the pawn. Your king gets there first, and nothing stops it now. Now watch your king close in. [%csl Ge5][%tail]} (3... Kf5 {Kf5 also wins: it reaches the pawn just as fast, coming from the other side.} 4. Kc1 Kf4 {Still winning: this brings you right back to the very same winning position.}) (3... Kd5 {Kd5 also heads toward the pawn, but it is one row too low to actually help.} 4. Kc3 {His king slips in front of the pawn on e3, and the win you had is gone.}) (3... Rd4 {Rd4 wanders the rook away and leaves your pawn with no guard at all.} 4. Rxe3 {The pawn on e3 is simply eaten by his rook, with nobody left to defend it. Drawn.}) 4. Kc1 4... Kf4 {Your king reaches f4, right beside the pawn. Your rook on e4 guards it from behind, his rook on e2 is nailed to the square in front of it, and his king on c1 is nowhere near. That is the rook behind the passed pawn, and you are winning. [%csl Gf4]} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: build the wall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/fdGo4RY7"]
[FEN "8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{A new word: cutting off. Build a wall with your rook, and his king can never cross to the other side of it. [%cal Yg2b2]}
1. Rb2 {Rb2 puts the wall up on the b column. His king is locked out on the wrong side of it for good. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb2b8]} (1. d6 {Running the pawn to d6 too soon starts the checks, and there is no wall left to shelter your king from them.} 1... Ra3+ 2. Ke2 Ra2+ {His checks never stop from there, and your king can never find safety. Drawn.}) (1. Rg7+ {Rg7+ is a check that chases his king nowhere useful, and it costs you the wall.} 1... Ka6 2. d6 Ra1 {His rook drops to a1, ready to check your king from behind, and his king is free to come back to the pawn. Drawn.}) (1. Ra2 {Offering the trade with Ra2 is one he is only too happy to take off the board.} 1... Rxa2 {His rook is left standing on the board, and yours is not. You are lost.}) (1. Rg4 {Rg4 steps the rook into the open, right where his rook can take it.} 1... Rxg4 {You are lost. His rook simply takes yours on g4 for nothing in return.}) (1. Kc3 {Walking the king up with Kc3 lets the checks arrive first, before your king is ready for them.} 1... Ra3+ 2. Kb2 Rd3 {He gets in front of the pawn on d6 before your king can help it. Drawn.}) 1... Rf4 {He shuffles his rook about behind the wall. Now walk your king up to join it. [%cal Yd3e3]} 2. Ke3 {Your king climbs, the wall still holds, and the pawn follows behind it. Now watch it walk home. [%csl Ge3][%tail]} (2. Kc3 {Kc3 also wins: your king can climb up either side of the wall on the b column.} 2... Rf3+ 3. Kc4 Rf4+ {Still winning: his checks slow you down, but they never catch your king.}) (2. Rb1 {Rb1 is also fine: the wall is a whole column wide, not one fixed square on it.} 2... Rf3+ {His checks keep coming from behind, but the wall on the b column still holds firm.}) (2. Rb5 {Rb5 also wins: the wall can slide to any square it likes up the b column.} 2... Ka6 {Still winning: the wall holds no matter which exact square on the column it sits on today.}) (2. d6 {Pushing to d6 too soon here needs the king's support first, not a head start alone.} 2... Rf6 3. d7 Rd6+ {He wins the pawn back on d6, and the position settles into a draw.}) (2. Rb7+ {Rb7+ is a check that simply gives the rook away to his own king.} 2... Kxb7 {You are lost. His king simply steps up and captures the rook that just checked it.}) (2. Ra2+ {Ra2+ trades the wall for one single check, and that is not a fair price.} 2... Kb6 3. d6 Rf1 {He is back in the game with his rook active again, and the position holds. Drawn.}) 2... Rc4 3. d6 3... Rc6 4. Rd2 4... Rc8 5. d7 5... Rd8 6. Ke4 6... Kb6 7. Ke5 7... Kc7 8. Ke6 8... Rh8 9. Rc2+ 9... Kb7 10. Rh2 10... Rxh2 11. d8=Q {A new queen stands on d8. He gave his rook for yours on h2, and the wall never once let his king across the b column.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: a wall on the fifth row"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/2jeUUw1L"]
[FEN "4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{His king is miles away in the corner, and his rook sits on e8. Cut him off.}
1. Rb5 {Rb5 builds a wall along the fifth row, running from a5 across to your own pawn on e5. His king can never climb over it. [%csl Gb5][%cal Ga5e5]} (1. e6 {Running the pawn to e6 alone sends it straight where his rook is already waiting.} 1... Rxe6 {The pawn is gone, eaten on e6. Drawn. It ran off with no king there to guard it.}) (1. Ra7+ {Ra7+ is a check, but once it is over the pawn on e5 still falls anyway.} 1... Kb3 2. e6 Rxe6 {Drawn. The check bought no time at all, and his rook simply eats the runner.}) (1. Kf3 {Bringing the king up with Kf3 leaves the pawn on e5 hanging with no rook behind it.} 1... Rxe5 {Drawn. Undefended, the pawn on e5 falls to his rook in a single move.}) (1. Rb2+ {Rb2+ steps the rook right next to his king, where it simply gets taken.} 1... Kxb2 {You are lost. His king simply captures the rook that just checked it.}) (1. Rb4 {Rb4 builds the wall on the fourth row, and it no longer guards your pawn on e5.} 1... Rxe5 {The pawn is gone. Drawn. His rook slips through the gap and takes it.}) 1... Ka3 {He wanders near his own corner on a3, going nowhere useful at all.} 2. Kf3 {Your king sets off, and the wall on the fifth row does the guarding by itself. [%csl Gf3]} (2. Kg3 {Kg3 also wins: any road toward the pawn is fine while the wall holds.} 2... Ka4 3. Rd5 {Still winning: the wall just slides along the same row, and it still runs from a5 across to your pawn on e5. [%cal Ga5e5]}) (2. Rd5 {Rd5 is also good, and you could just as well play this move a little later.} 2... Ka4 3. Kf3 {Still winning: your king strolls up while the wall guards the pawn all by itself.}) (2. Kf2 {Kf2 also wins: any road your king takes toward the pawn on e5 will do.} 2... Ka4 {Still winning: whichever path your king takes, it gets there just the same.}) (2. e6 {e6 still cannot run alone: the pawn needs an escort before it moves.} 2... Rxe6 {Drawn. It had no escort at all, and his rook simply eats it on e6.}) (2. Ra5+ {Ra5+ is a check that throws away the wall you had just spent moves building.} 2... Kb4 3. Ra1 Rxe5 {Drawn. Chasing the check let go of the wall, and the pawn falls.}) (2. Rb3+ {Rb3+ puts the rook just one step from his king, right where it gets taken.} 2... Kxb3 {You are lost. The check landed right next to his king, and it simply takes the rook.}) 2... Ka4 {He attacks your rook. Save it, but keep the wall standing.} 3. Rd5 {The rook is safe on d5, the wall still holds, and now it guards the pawn on e5 too. Now watch your king come up. [%csl Gd5][%tail]} (3. Rc5 {Rc5 is also good: the wall just needs to stay somewhere on the fifth row.} 3... Ka3 {Still winning: his king wanders about while the wall keeps doing its job.}) (3. e6 {Pushing with e6 here forgets that your rook on b5 was just attacked and left hanging.} 3... Kxb5 {The rook is gone. You are lost. His king simply eats the rook you left hanging.}) (3. Rb4+ {Rb4+ hands over the whole rook in exchange for just one single check.} 3... Kxb4 {Lost. You traded the rook for one check, and nothing is left to escort the pawn.}) 3... Kb3 4. Ke4 {Your king steps up beside the pawn on e4, and the rook on d5 cuts his king off down the whole d column — it can never reach the pawn at all. You are cutting him off and the pawn is escorted: winning. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd8d1]} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the defender builds one too"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/QrceVMS9"]
[FEN "4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His pawn on e4 wants to run, and his king on c3 is heading for your pawn on b5. Your job is to survive: one move stops both.}
1... Ra4 {Ra4 puts a wall on the fourth row. It blocks his pawn and his king at the very same time. [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga4h4]} (1... b4+ {Pushing with check on b4 feels great, but it loses everything you have.} 2. Kxb4 Ra7 3. Kc5 {Your pawn on b5 is gone, and his own pawn on e4 now runs free. Lost.}) (1... Ra3+ {A check with Ra3+ first, and the wall on the fourth row never gets built at all.} 2. Kb4 Ra7 3. e5 {His pawn on e4 gets clean away while you were busy checking. Lost.}) (1... Ra2 {Ra2 is too low a row to block anything: his pawn simply walks straight past it.} 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 {Lost. His rook slips behind your own pawn on b5 and stops it there cold.}) (1... Ra8 {Offering the trade with Ra8 turns this into a lost pawn ending straight away.} 2. Rxa8 b4+ 3. Kb3 {Lost. Without rooks on the board, his extra pawn just walks home.}) (1... Kc1 {Kc1 steps your king out and forgets about the pawn on b5 entirely.} 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 {Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn, and your king never came to help it.}) 2. e5 {His pawn on e4 walks right up to your wall on the fourth row. Now hold your ground.} 2... Re4 {You slide your rook along the wall and stand right in front of him. [%csl Ge4]} (2... b4+ {The same check again, and it costs you everything a second time.} 3. Kb3 Ra2 4. e6 {His pawn on e4 queens first, and there is nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (2... Rc4+ {Rc4+ is a check, but it gives up the wall on the fourth row you were holding.} 3. Kd3 Rc1 4. Rb8 {Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn now that yours has left the row.}) (2... Rb4 {Rb4 guards your own pawn on b5 instead of standing in his king's way.} 3. Kxb4 {The rook is gone, taken by his king with no support nearby. Lost.}) (2... Ra3+ {Ra3+ steps your rook off the fourth row, and his pawn on e4 runs free.} 3. Kb4 Re3 4. Ka5 {Lost. His king steps clear of the checks, and the pawn queens next.}) 3. e6 {He pushes past you, to e6. Now your own pawn is the one that saves you.} 3... b4+ {Now the check works: it drives his king away from your wall, and your pawn on b4 is suddenly a runner of its own. [%csl Gb4]} (3... Rxe6 {Grabbing the free pawn with Rxe6 lets his rook simply take yours right back.} 4. Rxe6 {Your rook is gone, and his pawn on e4 queens with nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (3... Re5 {Blocking with Re5 in front of the pawn, instead of checking, is the wrong idea.} 4. Kd4 {His king attacks your rook, and the pawn goes straight through. Lost.}) (3... Rc4+ {That is the wrong check: his king simply steps up and shelters beside the pawn.} 4. Kd3 {Lost. His king shelters right next to the pawn, and the check achieved nothing.}) 4. Kd2 {His king steps to d2. His pawn stands on e6, yours on b4. What now?} 4... b3 {Push! Your pawn on b3 is two steps from a queen, so his rook has to worry about it too. [%csl Gb3]} (4... Rc4 {Rc4 keeps your rook on the fourth row and waits, and that holds as well.} 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, but your rook can still drop to c7 in front of it. Still a draw.}) (4... Rd4+ {Rd4+ checks from the side, and that holds too.} 5. Ke1 {His king steps back to e1, and your b pawn is still ready to run. Still a draw.}) (4... Rf4 {Rf4 slides along your wall to f4 and holds the draw.} 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, and your rook can check from f2 or step up to f7 beside it. A draw.}) (4... Rg4 {Rg4 slides further along the wall, and it still holds.} 5. e7 {His pawn is on e7, but your rook on g4 can reach g7 and stop it. Drawn.}) (4... Rh4 {Rh4 goes to the far end of the wall and still holds.} 5. e7 {The pawn reaches e7, and your rook on h4 can still come to h7 in time. Drawn.}) (4... Re5 {Re5 steps back to stand right in front of his pawn, and that is a draw as well.} 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, but your rook on e5 still blocks its road. Drawn.}) (4... Kb2 {Kb2 walks your king up beside your own pawn, and that holds.} 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, and your pawn on b4 has its king right behind it now. Drawn.}) (4... Ka2 {Ka2 heads your king toward your pawn, and it still draws.} 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, but a king step to b2 or b3 keeps you in the race. Drawn.}) (4... Rxe6 {Grabbing the pawn on e6 walks straight into his rook on e8.} 5. Rxe6 {His rook takes yours on e6, and a lone pawn cannot fight a rook. Lost. [%cal Re8e6]}) (4... Re2+ {Re2+ puts your rook right beside his king.} 5. Kxe2 {His king takes your rook on e2, and your pawn is no match for his rook. Lost.}) (4... Ka1 {Ka1 hides your king in the corner, and his king has time to hunt your pawn.} 5. Kd3 Re1 6. Kc4 {His king reaches c4, your pawn on b4 falls next, and his pawn still stands on e6. Lost.}) 5. e7 {His pawn reaches e7, one step from a queen. Yours stands on b3. Your move.} 5... b2 {Push again! Your pawn on b2 queens next with check, so his rook has to give itself up for it. Now watch the race end. [%csl Gb2][%tail]} (5... Re5 {Re5 holds too: your rook stays in front of his pawn from the fifth row.} 6. Rb8 Rxe7 7. Rxb3+ {Each rook takes a pawn, and rook against rook is a draw.}) (5... Re6 {Re6 also stays in front of his pawn, one square higher, and it holds.} 6. Rb8 Rxe7 7. Rxb3+ {Both pawns come off the board, and rook against rook is drawn.}) (5... Kb2 {Kb2 brings your king beside your pawn, and that holds as well.} 6. Kd3 Re1 7. Kd2 {His king chases your rook, but your pawn on b3 is guarded and the game stays a draw.}) (5... Rxe7 {Taking on e7 now is one move too early: his rook on e8 takes back.} 6. Rxe7 Ka2 7. Kc3 {His rook is free, and his king walks up to your pawn on b3. Lost.}) (5... Rd4+ {Rd4+ checks, but his king steps to c3 and attacks your rook and pawn together.} 6. Kc3 Re4 7. Kxb3 {Your pawn on b3 is gone, and his pawn still stands on e7. Lost.}) 6. Kd3 6... Rxe7 7. Rxe7 7... Ka1 8. Re1+ 8... b1=Q+ 9. Rxb1+ 9... Kxb1 {Two bare kings on an empty board: a draw. Your wall on the fourth row stopped his pawn, and your own pawn did the rest. [%csl Gb1]} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: a busy rook beats a sleepy one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/GCG0qpGy"]
[FEN "8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{You are a pawn down and playing for the draw. A new word: the busy rook, a rook with a real job, like standing in front of his pawn. Yours sits idle on b4. [%cal Yb4b8]}
1... Rb8 {Rb8 leaves the king alone and takes the only job on the board that actually matters. [%csl Gb8]} (1... Rb2+ {Rb2+ checks, and checks again, but none of those checks slow the pawn down at all.} 2. Kf3 Rb3+ 3. Ke4 {The pawn just walks home to c8 with nothing there to trouble it. Lost.}) (1... Rb6 {Attacking the pawn from the side with Rb6 does nothing to actually stop it.} 2. Rc4+ Kb5 3. c7 {It reaches the last row and queens, with your rook still watching from the side. Lost.}) (1... Rb7 {Rb7 sits one row too high, right where the pawn wants to capture it.} 2. cxb7 {Lost. Your rook stood exactly on the square the pawn needed to capture on its way up.}) (1... Rc4 {Offering the trade with Rc4 hands him the whole race: a pawn runs faster with no rook in its way.} 2. Rxc4+ {Lost. Without rooks left on the board, the pawn simply runs home unopposed.}) (1... Kb3 {Kb3 wanders the king while the rook stays asleep on b4, doing nothing at all.} 2. Kf3 Rb8 3. Ke4 {Too slow: his king reaches the pawn before your king can ever help. Lost.}) 2. c7 {The pawn runs for the last row, on c7. Get your rook in front of it now.} 2... Rc8 {Rc8 is the only square in the world that stops it, and it holds the draw right there for good. [%csl Gc8]} (2... Rg8+ {Checking first with Rg8+ costs you the one move you needed to reach c8 in time.} 3. Kf3 Rc8 4. Ke4 {His king arrives at c8 in time to help the pawn queen. Lost.}) (2... Ra8 {Ra8 has the right row but the wrong column: it does not actually block anything.} 3. Rc4+ Ka5 4. Rc5+ {Lost. Your rook sat behind the pawn instead of in front of it, and his checks find your king instead.}) (2... Rb7 {Rb7 gets behind the pawn, but that is too late once it has reached the seventh row.} 3. Rc4+ {Lost. The check drives your king off the square, and the pawn queens right after.}) (2... Kb4 {Kb4 brings the king over but leaves the rook right where the pawn can eat it.} 3. cxb8=Q+ {His pawn takes your rook on b8 and becomes a queen there. Lost.}) 3. Rc1 {His rook lines up on c1, behind his pawn. Your move.} 3... Kb4 {Kb4 keeps your king close and leaves your busy rook on c8 exactly where it is. Now watch him try to break through. [%csl Gc8][%tail]} (3... Ka5 {Ka5 also holds: your king stays near, and your rook never leaves c8.} 4. Ra1+ Kb4 {His check chases your king back to b4, and the pawn on c7 still cannot move. Drawn.}) (3... Kb5 {Kb5 holds too, one row higher.} 4. Ra1 Rxc7 {His rook left the c column, so the pawn on c7 is yours for free. Drawn.}) (3... Rxc7 {Taking the pawn on c7 walks into his rook on c1, which guards it down the c column.} 4. Rxc7 {His rook takes yours on c7, and your bare king cannot stop his rook. Lost. [%cal Rc1c7]}) 4. Rb1+ 4... Ka4 {You step your king aside. His rook can check all day, but your busy rook never leaves c8 and his pawn never moves. Drawn.} 0-1


