{You are a pawn down and the draw is all you want, but his rook threatens mate on the back row. Find the move.}
1... Rf1+ {Rf1+ checks from the f column, five squares from his king, where he can never chase your rook down. A busy rook: now watch him try. [%csl Gf1][%tail]} (1... Ra7 {Ra7 blocks his rook on the seventh row, but the mate comes on the eighth.} 2. Rb8# {Mate! He could have taken your rook on a7, but mate is better: his king on f6 and pawn on e6 cover every escape square.}) (1... Ra6 {Ra6 attacks his pawn on e6, but it leaves your back row open.} 2. Rb8# {Mate! The rook drops down the b column to b8, and nothing can block it.}) (1... Ra8 {Blocking on the back row with Ra8 does nothing: he simply steps his king aside.} 2. Rg7 Kd8 3. Rg8+ {You are lost. The check drives your king off the row, and mate follows.}) (1... Kd8 {The king runs to d8, but his rook comes right along to keep chasing it.} 2. Rb8+ Kc7 3. Rf8 {Lost. His rook cuts yours off from the whole board, and it can never get back in time.}) (1... Rd1 {A check would have saved you here. Blocking with Rd1 stops nothing at all.} 2. Rb8+ Rd8 3. Rxd8+ {Lost. Blocking with the rook cost you the rook itself, as well as the whole game.}) 2. Kg6 2... Rg1+ 3. Kh6 3... Rh1+ {Check again, from h1. His king can run all it likes, but it can never hide from your busy rook and free the pawn at once. Drawn.} 0-1


