{A new word: the Philidor defence. Your king stays in front of his pawn, and your rook waits on the sixth row until he pushes past it. [%csl Gc8][%cal Gh6a6]}
1. c6 {He pushes his pawn past your rook, to c6. That is your signal: drop the rook right down to the first row. [%cal Yh6h1]} 1... Rh1 {Rh1 checks him from the first row forever now, and he can never hide from it. [%csl Gh1][%cal Gh1h5][%tail]} (1... Rh2 {Rh2 is also a draw: any square down on the first row gives the very same endless checks.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Still holding: your rook checks from the first row just the same.}) (1... Rh3 {Rh3 also holds: the rook is far enough from his king to check from behind.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Drawn. Every square on that row gives the same endless checks.}) (1... Rh4 {Rh4 is also fine: getting down to the first row is what matters, not exactly how far along it.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Drawn. His king can never escape a rook checking along an empty row.}) (1... Rxc6 {Grabbing the free pawn with Rxc6 turns a draw into a lost ending.} 2. Kxc6 Kd8 3. Ra7 {King and rook against your bare king now, with the pawn already gone too. Lost.}) (1... Rh5+ {Rh5+ checks from too close: his king simply walks straight at your rook.} 2. Kb6 Rd5 3. Rg8+ {Lost. Your rook stood too close to check from, and the pawn queens next.}) (1... Kd8 {Kd8 walks your king away from the pawn's own square, on c8, right when it needed to stay.} 2. Kb6 Ke8 3. Kb7 {The pawn queens. Lost. Nothing was left behind to stop it.}) (1... Rh8 {Dropping back with Rh8 has the right idea, but the first row was the target, not the eighth.} 2. Kb6 Kb8 3. Rb7+ {Lost. Your rook sits on the wrong row to give a single useful check.}) (1... Rh7 {Offering the trade with Rh7 is one he is only too happy to take off the board.} 2. Rxh7 {Lost. The rook that was holding the draw by itself is now simply gone.}) 2. Kb6 2... Rb1+ 3. Kc5 3... Rc1+ {Check again, and it never ends: his king can wander all day, and your rook keeps checking from behind. That is the Philidor defence. Drawn.} 0-1


