{His rook is checking you, and your pawn needs one more move to queen. Four legal moves here. Pick the right one.}
1. Rd5 {Rd5 blocks the check with your rook: a bridge, the rook shielding your king from his checks. [%csl Gd5]} (1. Kd6 {Kd6 also wins: your king walks straight out of the check, toward the pawn.} 1... Rh6+ {Still winning, though he can check you for a while before your king shakes him off.}) (1. Kb4 {Kb4 runs from the check and abandons the pawn completely.} 1... Re5 2. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. Unescorted on e8, the new queen falls to his rook at once.}) (1. Kc4 {Kc4 runs from the check too, and leaves the pawn on e7 alone.} 1... Re5 2. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. Undefended on e8, the new queen lasts only one move too.}) 1... Rh8 {He turns the trade down. His rook on h8 now guards e8, the pawn's queening square, so do not queen yet. [%cal Rh8e8]} 2. Re5 {Re5 tucks your rook in behind the pawn, and from e5 it guards the queening square on e8 as well. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5e8]} (2. Rf5 {Rf5 also wins: the rook swings to the f column, ready to guard the last row from f8.} 2... Kc7 3. Rf8 {Your rook covers e8 from f8, and the pawn queens next. Still winning. [%cal Gf8e8]}) (2. Rd6 {Rd6 wins too, though the rook guards nothing new there.} 2... Kc7 3. Kd5 {Your king steps up toward the pawn, and the win is still there, just slower.}) (2. e8=Q {Queening at once is the move everybody wants to play, but the new queen lands on e8 with nobody guarding her.} 2... Rxe8 {His rook takes her on e8 straight away. Rook against rook, and neither side can ever mate. Drawn.}) (2. Kd6 {Kd6 also wins: your king heads for the pawn and shelters it that way instead.} 2... Rh6+ 3. Kd7 {Still winning: the checks keep coming, but your king stays right beside the pawn on e7.}) (2. Rd8 {Rd8 also wins: it offers the trade again, this time on the last row.} 2... Rh5+ 3. Rd5 {Still winning, though you are back to offering the very same trade you began with.}) (2. Rd7+ {Rd7+ also wins: the check shoves his king one square further from the pawn.} 2... Ka6 3. Rd8 {Still winning: your rook takes the last row, and his king is too far off to help.}) (2. Rd4 {Rd4 steps the rook out of the pawn's column, where it guards nothing that matters.} 2... Kc7 3. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. With nothing behind the pawn, the new queen is taken the moment she arrives.}) (2. Kb5 {Walking the king away with Kb5 leaves the pawn on e7 with no escort at all.} 2... Kc7 3. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. His rook collects the queen on e8, and rook against rook is nobody's win.}) 2... Rc8+ {He swings round and checks you from the side instead.} 3. Kd6 {Kd6 steps your king out of the check and closer to the pawn on e7. Now watch the pawn go home. [%csl Gd6][%tail]} (3. Kd5 {Kd5 also wins: any step that keeps your king near the pawn does the job.} 3... Re8 4. Kd6 {Still winning: his rook blocks on e8, but your king walks up to drive it off.}) (3. Kd4 {Kd4 also wins, though it wanders one row further from the pawn than it needs to.} 3... Re8 4. Kd5 {Still winning: your king turns straight back toward the pawn on e7.}) (3. Kb4 {Kb4 runs the king right away from the pawn, and nothing is left to escort it.} 3... Re8 4. Re1 Kb6 {Drawn. His rook sits on e8 in front of the pawn, and your king is too far away to drive it off.}) (3. Kb5 {Kb5 walks away from the pawn on e7, and his king comes back to help.} 3... Re8 4. Re1 Kc7 {Drawn. His king comes back to help the rook, and the pawn on e7 is going nowhere.}) 3... Rc6+ 4. Kd5 4... Rc8 5. e8=Q {A new queen stands on e8, guarded by your rook on e5. The bridge on d5 stopped his checks, and trading her off would leave him a bare king against your rook. [%csl Ge8]} 1-0
